issues.body:
## Goal
Create one auditable lifecycle model across Fusion, Network, BackEnd and FrontEnd so production code, supported development tooling, experiments, generated artifacts and archived code are no longer mixed.

## Scope
- version a machine-readable component manifest with owner, status, entrypoints, deploy targets and tests;
- classify every tracked path as production, supported-dev, experimental, legacy-candidate, generated or documentation;
- define archive/removal rules and compatibility windows;
- add CI checks preventing undeclared production dependencies and generated artifacts from returning to `main`;
- coordinate the child remediation issues in all four repositories.

## Constraints
- do not move code merely because a directory is named `experimental`;
- prove non-use from build manifests, imports, runtime traces and CI before archival;
- preserve history through tags/archived branches or extracted archived repositories, not an `archive/` dumping ground on `main`;
- Network#247 owns native runtime migration; Network#257 owns the no-central-scheduler protocol.

## Acceptance
- every tracked path has an accountable lifecycle classification;
- production images/installers are built from explicit manifests;
- all archive candidates have evidence, rollback instructions and no production references;
- child issues are linked and ordered by dependency.

## Problem
Production Compose and application defaults currently include demo credential fallbacks and deployment-specific public IP/domain values. This permits insecure starts and makes environments non-portable.

## Remediation
- make mesh/TURN/JWT/database secrets mandatory in production with `${VAR:?required}` semantics;
- remove `demo-mesh`, test passwords and wildcard production CORS fallbacks;
- move public IPs, domains, ports and URLs to validated environment files;
- add startup validation that distinguishes development and production modes;
- rotate any value that has been used outside local development;
- add secret scanning and configuration tests without logging secret values.

## Acceptance
- production refuses to start with missing/default credentials;
- no tracked production file contains a deploy-specific public IP or literal secret fallback;
- development remains one-command through explicit `.example.env` values;
- Compose config and a production-negative test run in CI.

## Problem
The orchestrator image copies the complete Network checkout, while desktop packaging also copies broad directories. Tests, documentation, legacy prototypes and unrelated tools can therefore enter production artifacts.

## Work
- derive server and desktop manifests from the lifecycle inventory;
- copy/build only required modules, schemas, workloads and native binaries;
- replace broad `COPY Network/` and whole-directory bundling with explicit stages;
- generate an artifact bill of materials containing paths, versions, licenses and hashes;
- add negative checks for tests, coverage, viewer, caches, source-only tools and secrets;
- coordinate with Network#253 for the final no-Python artifact.

## Acceptance
- server image and desktop bundles have reviewed allowlists;
- runtime smoke tests pass from the reduced artifacts;
- SBOM/path manifest is published by CI;
- unexpected files fail the build rather than silently shipping.

## Context
Network#247 and Network#257 require replacing monolithic Python paths without breaking desktop, web, RPC, queue, signature, task-state or workload contracts.

## Work
- inventory all externally observed JSON/protobuf/HTTP/MCP/CLI/file contracts;
- create canonical fixtures and golden cross-language tests;
- characterize restart, cancellation, retries, result return, signatures, routing and task-state derivation;
- record current intentional behavior separately from known bugs;
- run old/new implementations against the same fixture corpus during dual-write migration.

## Rules
- no behavior refactor and global formatting in the same PR;
- no new central scheduler abstraction: Network#257 is normative;
- every removed legacy entrypoint requires an equivalent compatibility test or an explicit breaking-version decision.

## Acceptance
- compatibility matrix and golden fixtures are versioned;
- CI detects wire/storage/CLI drift;
- Network#248–#254 can migrate one boundary at a time with measurable parity.

## Problem
Core Python/CI checks are gated by an opt-in repository variable, while several languages lack standard format/lint/type gates. This allows main to advance without consistent validation.

## Work
- remove opt-in gating from required host-independent checks;
- add Ruff format/lint and Pyright for Python incrementally;
- enforce gofmt/go vet/staticcheck in BackEnd integration checks;
- add ESLint/Prettier/type checks for Vue/Nuxt/React packages;
- run cargo fmt/clippy/test, clang-format/clang-tidy where supported, ShellCheck/shfmt, PSScriptAnalyzer and actionlint;
- start with changed-file ratchets, then reduce the baseline deliberately;
- separate generated coverage publication from source commits.

## Acceptance
- every PR runs deterministic required checks without hidden variables;
- format-only changes are isolated from behavior changes;
- baseline debt is measurable and cannot increase;
- Windows/Linux-specific skips are explicit and reported.

## Goal
Reduce repository noise and agent context without deleting code still used by production, CI or compatibility tests.

## Candidates to prove
- committed coverage HTML/JSON/SVG and other regenerable reports;
- historical splitter/executor implementations identified by documentation;
- Qt viewer if no supported product/build consumes it;
- demo and fallback paths after dependency removal;
- obsolete Dockerfiles, fixtures and scripts discovered by the exhaustive inventory.

## Required evidence per candidate
- no import/build/package/deploy/runtime reference;
- last meaningful commit and owner review;
- replacement path and compatibility impact;
- tests passing after removal;
- recovery tag/branch or extracted archived repository.

## Acceptance
- generated reports are artifacts/Pages, not source commits;
- confirmed legacy code is absent from `main` and agent search context;
- active worker/executor/runtime code is not archived based on directory names alone;
- CI prevents removed generated paths from returning.

## Problem
The network registry handler mixes production filesystem paths, untyped JSON maps, aggregation, semantic-version logic and HTTP encoding. The server also uses direct `ListenAndServe` without explicit timeouts or graceful shutdown.

## Work
- introduce typed DTO/domain models for peers, tasks, workloads and responses;
- inject filesystem/repository paths through validated configuration;
- separate repository, service and HTTP handler layers;
- isolate semantic version comparison and terminal-status policy;
- add table-driven tests for malformed files, ordering, aggregation and versions;
- configure `http.Server` timeouts, signal cancellation and graceful shutdown;
- validate production CORS and remove wildcard credentialed fallback.

## Acceptance
- handlers contain no filesystem or aggregation implementation;
- public response contracts have typed tests;
- server shuts down cleanly and rejects unsafe production configuration;
- `gofmt`, `go vet`, tests and build are required in CI.

## Problem
`pages/dashboard.vue` owns navigation, job state, RPC task state, submission, cancellation, Solana wallet queries and charts in one large component. It contains explicit `any`, hard-coded chart data and response-type drift.

## Work
- define typed API contracts for jobs, network workloads and user/wallet data;
- extract `useJobs`, `useNetworkTasks`, `useCurrentUser` and `useSolanaBalance`;
- split sidebar, stats, job table, network table, submit modal and charts into components;
- centralize authenticated API client/error handling;
- remove unused options and demo data from production views;
- add component/composable tests, accessibility checks and responsive Playwright scenarios;
- add ESLint/Prettier/type-check gates.

## Acceptance
- dashboard page is an orchestration shell, not a business monolith;
- no explicit `any` at API boundaries;
- `workloads` response shape is typed and tested;
- no placeholder links or fabricated metrics appear as production data;
- unit, production build and functional tests pass.
